    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0181",
      "sequence": 193,
      "meaning": "The demons can change form and know every illusion; even in dreams they possess neither righteousness nor compassion. Sitting in council once, the naturally arrogant Rāvaṇa surveys his innumerable sons, followers, relatives and the boundless demon race, and speaks in anger and pride. The gods, he says, are their enemies but flee rather than face superior strength. Their one means of death is to disrupt every Brahmin’s meal, sacrifice, fire-offering and ancestral rite. Weakened by hunger and loss of power, the gods will readily come within reach; then Rāvaṇa will decide whether to kill or spare and subjugate them.",
